(
  $title$Brazilian service invoices: when to issue an NFS-e and what to check$title$,
  'brazilian-service-invoice-nfse-when-to-issue',
  $html$
<p class="lead"><strong>An NFS-e is the electronic fiscal document used to record a service transaction in Brazil.</strong> It identifies the provider, customer, service, amount, and tax information. MEI service providers have used the national issuer mandatorily since September 1, 2023. ME and EPP companies enrolled in Simples Nacional have also been required to use it since September 1, 2026. Outside those cases, the issuing environment and fields can depend on municipal law and activity. This article is educational, not definitive advice.</p>

<p>Correct issuance helps prove revenue, meet business customer requirements, and keep financial records consistent. The main precaution is not treating every service in the same way. ISS location, service codes, withholding, deductions, rates, and deadlines can vary. Confirm the tax setup and applicable rules before automating issuance.</p>

<h2 id="what-is">What is an NFS-e in Brazil?</h2>
<p>NFS-e means Nota Fiscal de Serviço eletrônica, or electronic service invoice. It records a service subject to Brazil's municipal service tax framework, known as ISS. A city system or the national environment generally receives transaction data and authorizes the document. Complementary Law 116/2003 establishes national ISS principles and a service list, while municipalities regulate the tax within their powers.</p>

<p>An invoice is not a work order and it is not a payment slip. A <a href="/en/os-digital">digital work order</a> records the request, execution, materials, photos, and acceptance. The fiscal document records the taxable transaction. Payment settles the receivable. These records can be linked, but they have different purposes.</p>

<table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Main purpose</th><th>Typical stage</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Quote</td><td>Present scope, price, and terms</td><td>Before approval</td></tr>
<tr><td>Work order</td><td>Organize and evidence execution</td><td>During service</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFS-e</td><td>Record the service for fiscal purposes</td><td>According to the applicable rule</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment request or receipt</td><td>Request or evidence payment</td><td>According to commercial terms</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h2 id="nfse-nfe">What is the difference between NFS-e and NF-e?</h2>
<p>NFS-e is associated with services. NF-e model 55 is mainly used for goods and other transactions governed by state rules. Businesses providing materials and services in one contract may face specific treatment. Do not select a fiscal document only from the wording in a quote. The real nature of the transaction, the agreement, and tax rules must agree.</p>

<p>Maintenance, installation, and construction work can involve special rules for materials, tax base, and withholding. Analysis may depend on activity, city, state, and contract. Validate uncertain cases before issuance. Correcting a document later may require cancellation or replacement through the authorized procedure.</p>

<h2 id="when">When must a Brazilian service provider issue an NFS-e?</h2>
<p>The safe answer is to issue whenever applicable law requires it and at the event defined by that law. Since September 1, 2026, ME and EPP businesses under Simples Nacional must use the National Issuer. Other taxpayers should consult the municipal authority, local code, and accountant. Substantive ISS rules still vary, and schedules can change.</p>

<p>Do not confuse service date, tax competence, issue date, and payment due date. They can differ. Competence identifies the period of the service; issuance creates the document; due date belongs to collection. Completing a work order should not automatically issue an invoice unless fiscal data and the issue event have been validated.</p>

<h3 id="mei">When does a MEI service provider issue an NFS-e?</h3>
<p>The official government service states that a MEI is generally exempt from issuing an invoice to an individual consumer unless that consumer requests it. When the customer is a legal entity, issuance is mandatory. Since September 1, 2023, a MEI service provider uses the national standard regardless of the previous municipal system. Always review the official portal because procedures and manuals can be updated.</p>

<blockquote><p>Practical MEI summary: services to a company require an invoice. Services to an individual are generally exempt, but the document must be issued when requested.</p><p><a href="https://www.gov.br/pt-br/servicos/emitir-nota-fiscal-de-servico-eletronica" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the official NFS-e service page</a></p></blockquote>

<h3 id="other-companies">What about a microenterprise or Simples Nacional company?</h3>
<p>Being classified as ME does not create an NFS-e exemption. ME is a business size classification, while Simples is a tax regime. Since September 1, 2026, ME and EPP companies under Simples Nacional must use the National Issuer. They must still confirm registration, service code, ISS rules, special regimes, withholding, and starting date.</p>

<h2 id="data">Which data should be checked before issuance?</h2>
<p>Collect provider, customer, service, and tax data. A commercial contact record may not contain all fiscal information. A trading name can differ from the registered legal name. Validate the CNPJ or CPF, address when required, municipality, delivery email, and service identification.</p>

<table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>What to review</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Provider</td><td>CNPJ, municipal registration, regime, and establishment</td><td>Rejection or inconsistent taxation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Customer</td><td>Tax ID, legal name, city, and contact</td><td>Document issued to the wrong party</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service</td><td>Description, code, competence, location, and amount</td><td>Wrong classification or jurisdiction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taxes</td><td>ISS, withholding, rate, deductions, and other fields</td><td>Incorrect return or payment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Links</td><td>Contract, work order, purchase order, and receivable</td><td>Weak reconciliation and evidence</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h2 id="steps">How can you issue an NFS-e safely?</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Confirm the environment.</strong> MEI and Simples Nacional businesses use the National Issuer on their mandatory dates. Other taxpayers check the applicable environment.</li>
<li><strong>Review registration.</strong> Confirm municipal registration, regime, activities, and authorization before the first issue.</li>
<li><strong>Validate the customer.</strong> Use tax data confirmed by the customer, not only a phone contact.</li>
<li><strong>Classify the service.</strong> Map the commercial description to the correct fiscal code instead of blindly copying a previous document.</li>
<li><strong>Enter competence and location.</strong> These may affect assessment and must represent the actual transaction.</li>
<li><strong>Review values and withholding.</strong> Compare them with the contract, work order, and tax guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Issue and keep the result.</strong> Store the number, status, XML or available representation, and protocol.</li>
<li><strong>Reconcile records.</strong> Link the invoice to the receivable and record cancellation, replacement, or payment without deleting history.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="description">How should the service description be written?</h2>
<p>The description should explain the service without exposing unnecessary information. Use objective language, a contract or work order reference when useful, the period, and location when required. Avoid empty wording such as “services rendered,” and do not paste sensitive details from a technical report.</p>

<p>Example: “Preventive maintenance of an air-conditioning system, October 2026 visit, under work order 1234.” This example does not determine the fiscal code or tax jurisdiction. Those fields depend on the real classification. A clear commercial description cannot correct an incorrect tax code.</p>

<h2 id="iss">Is ISS always due in the provider's city?</h2>
<p>No. Complementary Law 116 sets a general rule and exceptions where tax may be due elsewhere. Treatment depends on the service and facts. Companies with field teams in several cities should not assume that every invoice has the same ISS jurisdiction.</p>

<p>ISS withholding is not the same as a tax exemption. If the customer withholds tax, it may be responsible for payment, but both parties still need correct invoice and return data. Confirm local rules, the contract, and customer registration before issuance.</p>

<h2 id="mistakes">Which mistakes create the most rework?</h2>
<ul>
<li>Issuing to the wrong CNPJ because the requester and payer are different entities.</li>
<li>Reusing an old service code without confirming the current scope.</li>
<li>Confusing a commercial discount with an allowed deduction from the tax base.</li>
<li>Marking withholding without checking the agreement and law.</li>
<li>Issuing twice after an uncertain processing response.</li>
<li>Cancelling without updating the receivable, accounting, and work order link.</li>
<li>Editing a PDF instead of using the issuer's official correction procedure.</li>
</ul>

<p>Integrations should treat issuance idempotently: retrying a request must not generate two invoices for one transaction. Before resending after a failure, query the status using the available identifier. Keep useful logs without exposing credentials or unnecessary personal data.</p>

<h2 id="cancel">How do cancellation and replacement work?</h2>
<p>Procedure and deadline depend on the issuer and local rules. Some cases allow direct cancellation, while others require replacement or municipal review. Never delete the invoice from internal records. Keep the reason, date, protocol, and replacement document, then update accounts receivable and accounting.</p>

<p>If the mistake affects amount, customer, competence, or taxation, confirm the proper procedure before acting. An informal note may not correct a fiscal field. The issuer's support channel, municipal authority, and accountant are the right references.</p>

<h2 id="management">How should NFS-e connect to service management?</h2>
<p>A sound workflow links the contract, work order, fiscal document, and collection while preserving separate statuses. A completed work order can indicate readiness for fiscal review. An authorized invoice releases the document to the customer. Payment settles the receivable. Fiscal cancellation must not erase the completed job.</p>

<p>The Dominex <a href="/en/emissao-de-nfse">NFS-e issuing page</a> explains the platform's fiscal integration. Availability and requirements depend on plan, municipality, provider, and registration. Automation reduces typing, but it does not replace a correct tax definition.</p>

<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions about NFS-e</h2>
<h3>Does every service provider issue NFS-e?</h3><p>There is no single answer for every case. MEI has specific national rules. Other businesses must check municipal law, activity, customer, and regime.</p>
<h3>Does a MEI issue to an individual?</h3><p>The national FAQ generally provides an exemption unless the individual requests the document. Issuance to a legal entity is mandatory.</p>
<h3>Can one monthly invoice cover several jobs?</h3><p>Only when compatible with the agreement and applicable rules. Competence, customer, and detail must accurately represent the transactions.</p>
<h3>Does payment determine the invoice date?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Performance, competence, issuance, and payment are separate events. Confirm the applicable rule.</p>
<h3>Does software remove the need for an accountant?</h3><p>No. Software executes configured records and integrations. Classification, codes, withholding, and obligations require responsible professional definition.</p>

<div style="margin:28px 0;padding:22px;border-radius:14px;background:#073b33;color:#fff"><h2 style="margin-top:0;color:#fff">Connect the job, invoice, and receivable</h2><p>Explore Dominex to connect field operations with fiscal and financial management while applying the rules configured for your company.</p><p><a href="/en/planos" style="display:inline-block;padding:12px 18px;border-radius:9px;background:#00C597;color:#fff;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none">View plans</a></p></div>

<h2 id="references">Official references</h2><ul>
<li><a href="https://www.gov.br/nfse/pt-br/mei-prestadores-de-servico-de-todo-o-pais-estao-obrigados-a-emitir-nfs-e" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National NFS-e portal: national standard for MEI</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.gov.br/pt-br/servicos/emitir-nota-fiscal-de-servico-eletronica" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gov.br: issue a national-standard NFS-e</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.gov.br/nfse/pt-br/noticias/nfs-e-e-simples-nacional-obrigatoriedade-de-emissao-atraves-do-emissor-nacional" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NFS-e portal: National Issuer requirement for Simples Nacional</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/leis/lcp/lcp116.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Complementary Law 116/2003</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.gov.br/nfse/pt-br/mei-e-demais-empresas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National NFS-e portal: issuers and service list</a></li>
</ul>
  $html$,
  $excerpt$Understand Brazilian NFS-e rules for MEI and other service companies, what to check before issue, and how to avoid ISS, withholding, and duplicate errors.$excerpt$,
  'Tips', ARRAY['Brazilian NFS-e','service invoice Brazil','MEI invoice','ISS tax','service provider','fiscal management'],
  'published', 'Dominex',
